it's an almost completely anonymous  building in central caracas but it is a  high security prison  no outsiders have ever been allowed in  here before  we've searched in our equipment checked  before guards opened the gate  the multi-story building is basically a  huge square  the staircase leads you to the only  outside space in the prison  this secretive institution has a special  class of  inmate this is where the venezuelans  lock up the  [Music]  these foreigners parade ground rituals  are not compulsory  but if the prisoners don't comply to  what is a form of institutionalized  brainwashing  then they're given no good behavior  credits against their sentences  the foreigners are praising the  revolution of hugo chavez  and they're from all over the world  they're almost all  convicted drug smugglers  salvatore cherelli is from southampton  his father is italian  he's serving 13 years for cocaine  smuggling  he admits trafficking several times he  was he says  trying to raise money to come home to  the uk  we went broke so we decided i decided  not that i decided to do that so  it was uh to get more money to go to  india because we don't have money enough  to go to england to survive in england  so we have to sell the houses here  the cars the lands and everything to go  back to europe  this is by no means the worst prison in  latin america  but for the men spending long hours in  tiny cells  the fact that they're in a country where  ordinary people can't eat  where medical supplies are scarce and  human rights are regularly abused  means they're extremely vulnerable the  prison system like everything else in  venezuela  is in danger of falling apart with them  inside  they have no communication with the  outside world  in the culture room basically the  library john tweel  looks after the prison books originally  from spalding the 58 year old  is serving 15 years caught with 31 kilos  of cocaine at the airport  like most of the prisoners any despair  has long since gone  when you've been in prison eight years  eight and a half years it's no good  crying i'm gonna  cry what would be sad to be sad i'm sick  as it is i can't it's no good  me getting down and depressed because  i'll get sick more sick soon  so it's no good getting depressed about  these things and hopefully my sentence  is finished soon  alexis sanchez is from new jersey in the  united states  a man in his hostel room was carrying  drugs he wasn't he claims  either way he's now serving 17 years for  dealing  mr sanchez wants to be repatriated to  the united states  but with relations between the u.s and  venezuela at an all-time low  he knows his case is not a priority  maybe because of my nationality  they could disapprove this is just an  opinion that i'm having it's not a fact  you understand  but we all know that in politics these  type of things  tend to happen from time to time  the venezuelan authorities confirm that  despite covid all the foreigners remain  in this prison  and are alive all the repatriation  requests  appear to be suspended but there is no  other communication with the men  and of course they have no idea that a  pandemic  has just swept across the world stuart  ramsey sky news  caracas
 

